@page "/Home"

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Rig</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice manages Rig records: the static master data describing a drilling installation, its
    classification, rated operating envelope, marine/jack-up/station-keeping profiles, drilling and well-control
    equipment, and extensible feature assignments. It persists to SQLite, enforces optimistic concurrency on every
    mutation, and exposes the same operations through a REST API, an MCP server, and a Blazor web application, so a
    rig record created or edited in one interface is immediately visible through the others.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a Rig</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>Rig</b> is the master-data record for one drilling installation: a name, a description,
                server-owned creation and modification timestamps, structured <b>identification</b> (owner, operator,
                manufacturer or shipyard, year built, classification society, external identifiers), a
                classification, a rated <b>operating envelope</b>, and the mast, mud, well-control, and
                managed-pressure-drilling equipment installed on it.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Rig records describe static equipment specifications, certified limits, and structured
                instrumentation — not live measurements or changing controller state, which are intentionally kept
                outside this service. Rig UUIDs are caller-generated, so a consuming system can mint and reference an
                identifier before the record exists on the server.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Fixed and mobile rigs</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Every rig carries a <code>RigType</code> (for example <i>ConventionalLandRig</i>, <i>JackUp</i>,
                <i>Semisubmersible</i>, <i>Drillship</i>, <i>TenderAssistedRig</i>, <i>DrillingBarge</i>), an
                <code>OperatingEnvironment</code> (<i>Onshore</i>, <i>Offshore</i>, <i>InlandWater</i>), and a
                <code>MobilityType</code> (<i>Fixed</i>, <i>Mobile</i>, <i>SelfPropelled</i>,
                <i>NonSelfPropelled</i>). Together they say what the installation is and how it gets to location,
                whether it is a land rig moved between pads, a self-propelled drillship, or a platform rig fixed in
                place for the life of the field.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The older <code>IsFixedPlatform</code> boolean is kept as a compatibility flag for existing clients;
                <code>RigType</code> is authoritative for new records. Marine units, jack-ups, and station-keeping
                systems carry their own dedicated profile objects rather than overloading the base rig fields.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Rigs and clusters</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A fixed-platform rig belongs to a <b>Cluster</b> — the platform, template, or pad it is permanently
                mounted on — referenced by <code>ClusterID</code>, an external UUID owned by the Cluster
                microservice. Rig writes enforce the relationship: a fixed-platform rig requires a non-empty,
                live-verified <code>ClusterID</code>, while a non-fixed rig (a mobile land rig, a self-propelled
                drillship) must leave it null.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The Cluster reference is verified against the live Cluster service before a rig is created or
                replaced; an unavailable Cluster service rejects the write rather than silently accepting an
                unverifiable reference or leaving Rig data inconsistent.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Drill floor elevation as a depth reference</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                <code>DrillFloorElevation</code> records how high the rig's drill floor sits above the vertical
                datum used at its location. Depths measured while drilling — measured depth, true vertical depth —
                are naturally referenced to the drill floor, which is a moving target from one rig to the next; two
                wells drilled from rigs with different drill floor elevations are not directly comparable on that
                basis alone.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Combining a rig's drill floor elevation with its Cluster's location and the Earth Vertical Datum
                service resolves a common <b>mean-sea-level depth reference</b>, so that Well and WellBore data tied
                to this rig can be expressed on a consistent vertical datum regardless of which rig drilled them.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Conventions</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Physical quantities are stored in <b>SI units</b> throughout the model and API; the web application's
        unit-aware fields convert to and from the unit system selected for the page through the OSDC
        unit-reference system, so a value can be entered and displayed in field units while the service always
        persists SI. Enumerations such as <code>RigType</code> and <code>MobilityType</code> serialize as exact
        strings rather than numeric codes, so payloads stay self-describing across REST, MCP, and stored JSON.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Every update, replacement, or delete uses <b>optimistic concurrency</b>: the caller echoes back the
        <code>LastModificationDate</code> from its latest read, and a stale write is rejected with HTTP 409 rather
        than silently overwriting a concurrent change. The service preserves <code>CreationDate</code> and assigns a
        fresh <code>LastModificationDate</code> on every successful write.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Rig <b>features</b> are named capabilities drawn from seven immutable built-in catalogs plus any number of
        user-created categories and options, assigned to a rig with optional exclusivity within a category and an
        optional validity period; built-in and still-referenced definitions cannot be deleted. One or more JPEG,
        PNG, or WebP <b>photographs</b> can be attached to a rig — up to 10 MiB each, checksummed with SHA-256, and
        stored apart from the Rig JSON so ordinary reads stay lightweight.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/Rig/webapp/Rig">Rig</MudLink> to browse and search the rig catalog; every row opens
        directly in the single create/edit workflow covering identification, classification, operating envelope,
        marine/jack-up/station-keeping profiles, storage capacities, feature assignments, the photo gallery, and the
        complete mast and equipment trees, with tree-based navigation of the nested object graph.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Manage the shared feature catalog — built-in and custom categories and options — on
        <MudLink Href="/Rig/webapp/Rig/Features">Rig Features</MudLink>, move data between installations on
        <MudLink Href="/Rig/webapp/Rig/BackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink>, and review per-endpoint request
        counts and daily history on <MudLink Href="/Rig/webapp/StatisticsRig">Usage Statistics</MudLink>. All numeric
        fields follow the unit system selected for the page.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From
        <MudLink Href="/Rig/webapp/Rig/BackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink>, export <b>all</b> rigs or an
        ordered <b>selection</b> into one versioned document that carries the complete rigs together with the
        Cluster UUID/name manifest and Rig Feature definitions they actually reference, and the complete photograph
        content — enough to reproduce the rigs on another installation without pulling in unrelated catalog clutter.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring reconnects Cluster references by exact UUID or a single unique normalized-name match, maps or
        creates local feature definitions according to policy, and commits catalog changes, rigs, and photos in one
        SQLite transaction, so a restore of many rigs either completes fully or leaves the database untouched.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same rig data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying store, so it can be
        managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, backing up, and
                    restoring rigs, managing the feature catalog, and reviewing usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/Rig/api</code> for rig CRUD, lightweight and metadata-only discovery,
                    feature-category catalog management, photo upload and content retrieval, and batch export/restore,
                    documented and explorable through the Swagger UI at <code>/Rig/api/swagger</code>. Suited to
                    scripts, backend integration, and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at <code>/rig/api/mcp</code> (and
                    WebSocket at <code>/rig/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing 18 underscore-only tools — every
                    non-statistics Rig and Rig Feature Category operation plus connectivity <code>ping</code> — with
                    strict input and success-output JSON Schemas, human-readable titles, and behavior annotations, so
                    AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can query, create, update, and back up rigs directly. Binary photo
                    content is excluded from ordinary MCP reads; the batch-export tool is the sole exception, since a
                    recoverable backup must contain the photographs.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the Rig, feature-catalog, and backup/restore pages are published as
                    the <code>OSDC.Drilling.Rig.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other Blazor applications — such as
                    the Well and WellBore web apps, which show Rig as contextual data — can embed the same
                    unit-aware controls instead of re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
